{{also|VOX|VOx}}
==Latin==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|ine-pro|la}} {{recons|wōkʷs||speech, voice|lang=ine-pro}}. Cognates include {{etyl|sa|-}} {{term|sc=Deva|वाच्|tr=vā́c|lang=sa}} and {{etyl|grc|-}} {{term|sc=polytonic|ὄψ|tr=óps|lang=grc}}. Proto-Indo-European {{recons|wōkʷ-|lang=ine-pro}} is an [[o-grade]] of {{recons|wekʷ-|lang=ine-pro}}, which is where {{etyl|grc|-}} {{term|ἔπος|tr=épos|lang=grc}} came from.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|Classical}} {{IPA|/woːks/|lang=la}}
* {{audio|la-cls-vox.ogg|Audio (Classical)}}

===Noun===
{{la-noun|vōx|vocis#Latin|vōcis|f|third}}

# [[voice]]
# [[accent]]
# [[speech]], [[remark]]
# {{grammar|lang=la}} [[voice]]; indicating the relation of the subject of the verb to the action which the verb expresses
# {{inflection of|lang=la|vox||voc|s}}

